131
00:10:25,393 --> 00:10:30,316
All right, so
now getting back to the selection

132
00:10:30,316 --> 00:10:34,510
process of which one would AS1 choose.

133
00:10:34,510 --> 00:10:39,568
Another kind of component
of this is we saw this

134
00:10:39,568 --> 00:10:45,017
abstraction or
we're making the assumption that

135
00:10:45,017 --> 00:10:49,960
each autonomous system
was a single router.

136
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:51,658
But in reality, these are big networks.

137
00:10:51,658 --> 00:10:53,523
They're going to be many, many routers.

138
00:10:53,523 --> 00:10:58,654
And so shown here,
AS2 simply has two routers.

139
00:10:58,654 --> 00:11:03,278
So one question or
the question shown here is when this

140
00:11:03,278 --> 00:11:08,230
router on the right inside
of AS2 received that update?

141
00:11:08,230 --> 00:11:13,301
Well, how did the router on
the left find out about it

142
00:11:13,301 --> 00:11:19,073
like now we have this new path and
update the routing table?

143
00:11:19,073 --> 00:11:24,791
And so what actually happens internally,
BGP is also run.

144
00:11:24,791 --> 00:11:30,238
And so when it's internal which
is basically just exchanging

145
00:11:30,238 --> 00:11:35,369
between all of these routers,
all of the information that

146
00:11:35,369 --> 00:11:41,233
they learned externally between
each other, that's called i for

147
00:11:41,233 --> 00:11:46,281
internal, iBGP and
then the external part of it is eBGP.

148
00:11:46,281 --> 00:11:51,346
Another thing,
another aspect to learn about for

149
00:11:51,346 --> 00:11:56,540
BGP is well,
how did AS4 learn about this prefix?

150
00:11:56,540 --> 00:12:00,026
We see it in the diagram, but
how did it actually learn about it?

151
00:12:00,026 --> 00:12:06,224
So it could have learned it about it
through a local protocol say, OSPF.

152
00:12:06,224 --> 00:12:12,412
So maybe there's a router here
in AS4 that's participating

153
00:12:12,412 --> 00:12:17,648
in BGP with neighbors in
other autonomous systems,

154
00:12:17,648 --> 00:12:22,418
but it's also participating
in OSPF locally.

155
00:12:22,418 --> 00:12:27,740
And so that's how all of these prefixes,
these link states

156
00:12:27,740 --> 00:12:33,281
that it learned about,
it can now announce to its neighbors.

157
00:12:33,281 --> 00:12:34,868
Another option is potentially,

158
00:12:34,868 --> 00:12:37,593
you just configured the router
with some static routes.

159
00:12:37,593 --> 00:12:44,474
You added prefix D to be something that
AS4, the router in it should announce.

160
00:12:47,168 --> 00:12:53,559
All right, so that's how do
you learn about the prefixes.

161
00:12:53,559 --> 00:12:59,105
Another aspect of BGP which
makes it highly valuable and

162
00:12:59,105 --> 00:13:03,825
useful in the internet is
the Internet is really

163
00:13:03,825 --> 00:13:08,906
a collection of companies,
trying to make money.

164
00:13:08,906 --> 00:13:10,206
It's a business.

165
00:13:10,206 --> 00:13:13,663
And so businesses need policies.

166
00:13:13,663 --> 00:13:17,176
There's different business
arrangements that occur.

167
00:13:17,176 --> 00:13:22,403
And so there's kind of three
main business arrangements

168
00:13:22,403 --> 00:13:26,304
that two autonomous systems would have.

169
00:13:26,304 --> 00:13:30,020
So one, you would have a provider.

170
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:35,338
This is companies that are paid
money to transit traffic.

171
00:13:35,338 --> 00:13:39,771
You have the customer that
pays money to a provider to

172
00:13:39,771 --> 00:13:44,014
have his traffic carried and
then you have a peer.

173
00:13:44,014 --> 00:13:48,574
And this is a financially neutral
arrangement where two **** connect to

174
00:13:48,574 --> 00:13:52,062
each other and
will send traffic through each other.

175
00:13:52,062 --> 00:13:56,592
And it's typically,
they're about the same size,

176
00:13:56,592 --> 00:14:03,146
same amount of traffic and it's kind of
making it, so that neither has to pay.

177
00:14:03,146 --> 00:14:09,018
And of course, any given autonomous
system can have both relationships.

178
00:14:09,018 --> 00:14:13,360
So not shown, but say AS1 needs to use

179
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:17,845
a bigger provider to get to some areas.

180
00:14:17,845 --> 00:14:22,626
And so
it would have AS300 as its customer,

181
00:14:22,626 --> 00:14:27,163
but then let's say AS400 not shown, but

182
00:14:27,163 --> 00:14:32,690
imagine it kind of above
AS100 as its own provider.

183
00:14:32,690 --> 00:14:40,092
And so what we need is policies to be
able to control what's being announced.

184
00:14:40,092 --> 00:14:44,043
If I'm paying some other company, so

185
00:14:44,043 --> 00:14:50,093
AS3 is 300 is paying AS100
money to carry traffic,

186
00:14:50,093 --> 00:14:55,526
AS100 shouldn't be able
to send traffic through

187
00:14:55,526 --> 00:15:00,604
AS300 to get it to say something in AS200.

188
00:15:00,604 --> 00:15:07,317
Because then, that's not making
sense financially for AS300.

189
00:15:07,317 --> 00:15:11,832
So in this case, we need some policies and

190
00:15:11,832 --> 00:15:15,967
BGP has this ability to set policies.

191
00:15:15,967 --> 00:15:20,782
And to understand that,
you can look at what

192
00:15:20,782 --> 00:15:26,379
the actual processing of
BGP messages look like and

193
00:15:26,379 --> 00:15:29,778
it's a processing pipeline.

194
00:15:29,778 --> 00:15:34,482
So these are the connections to peers and
these are just TCP

195
00:15:34,482 --> 00:15:39,186
sessions which we'll learn
about in the next module, but

196
00:15:39,186 --> 00:15:45,525
it's a session that you're getting
messages, these route announcements.

197
00:15:45,525 --> 00:15:50,124
You're putting it in a table of things
that you learned from that peer.

198
00:15:50,124 --> 00:15:54,290
You have an interface where you
can specify some policies and

199
00:15:54,290 --> 00:15:56,168
these are filters saying,

200
00:15:56,168 --> 00:16:00,670
I don't want to receive these types
of messages from these peers.

201
00:16:00,670 --> 00:16:06,125
So it filters those before,
it goes into a selection process.

202
00:16:06,125 --> 00:16:11,706
Then that selection process
puts all this information

203
00:16:11,706 --> 00:16:16,083
into the local routing information base.

204
00:16:16,083 --> 00:16:21,525
So this is just a database of all
the routes that you've learned and

205
00:16:21,525 --> 00:16:24,247
down into the forwarding table,

206
00:16:24,247 --> 00:16:29,026
which are the ones specifically
that were chosen as best.

207
00:16:29,026 --> 00:16:34,300
All right, now for the Egress side,
what you're announcing.

208
00:16:34,300 --> 00:16:38,761
So you've chosen something
as a best route.

209
00:16:38,761 --> 00:16:41,928
Well, now it goes through
your output policies.

210
00:16:41,928 --> 00:16:47,172
These are kind of Egress filters
that then get propagated.

211
00:16:47,172 --> 00:16:52,071
So maybe it's in the case of the consumer,
well, it doesn't want to

212
00:16:52,071 --> 00:16:57,057
announce things to AS100 anything
that it learned from AS200,

213
00:16:57,057 --> 00:17:00,877
so that AS100 wouldn't
send traffic through it.

214
00:17:00,877 --> 00:17:08,178
So it sets an output policy, a filter to
make it, so that that's not possible.

215
00:17:08,178 --> 00:17:15,716
So now with all that context,
this is how we calculate a best path.

216
00:17:15,716 --> 00:17:22,722
This is how BGP, a given router chooses
which route to choose as is best and

217
00:17:22,722 --> 00:17:29,391
it's basically this kind of prioritized
list that it just goes down.

218
00:17:29,391 --> 00:17:32,518
So looks at a thing
called local preference.

219
00:17:32,518 --> 00:17:37,892
So a network administrator could come
in and put a little tag on something.

220
00:17:37,892 --> 00:17:40,767
It says, I actually prefer this route.

221
00:17:40,767 --> 00:17:46,727
So it's kind of that manual fudging, but
that would take the highest preference.

222
00:17:46,727 --> 00:17:52,825
And so it's a numerical value that's
assigned by some routing policy.

223
00:17:52,825 --> 00:17:56,139
The next would be the AS path length.

224
00:17:56,139 --> 00:18:01,637
And so we saw this the AS3 and
AS2 were choosing

225
00:18:01,637 --> 00:18:07,003
the length one path over
the length two paths.

226
00:18:07,003 --> 00:18:12,128
All right, so that would be
the next kind of decision point.

227
00:18:12,128 --> 00:18:15,343
After that, if the paths are equal,

228
00:18:15,343 --> 00:18:19,407
the next thing to look at
is a field called MED.

229
00:18:19,407 --> 00:18:20,576
We didn't really get into this.

230
00:18:20,576 --> 00:18:24,470
It's called the multiple
exit discriminator and

231
00:18:24,470 --> 00:18:30,030
this is the case where if you have
kind of two exit points, a neighboring

232
00:18:30,030 --> 00:18:35,701
AS can tell you this is the one I would
prefer you to send traffic through.

233
00:18:35,701 --> 00:18:40,393
And so
that's another component of this decision.

234
00:18:40,393 --> 00:18:47,748
The next is it would
choose eBGP over iBGP.

235
00:18:47,748 --> 00:18:52,147
So stuff that you've
learned directly router,

236
00:18:52,147 --> 00:18:59,444
it would choose that over things that it's
learning through the iBGP process and

237
00:18:59,444 --> 00:19:03,536
then the next would be
the shortest IGP path.

238
00:19:03,536 --> 00:19:11,116
So IGP is just referring to
the internal kind of routing protocols.

239
00:19:11,116 --> 00:19:16,989
So OSPF, if there was a shortest
path learned through OSPF,

240
00:19:16,989 --> 00:19:20,106
it's going to kind of choose that.

241
00:19:20,106 --> 00:19:27,213
And then finally, have an arbitrary
tiebreaker of some arbitrary router ID.

242
00:19:27,213 --> 00:19:31,101
All right, so now we know kind of these

243
00:19:31,101 --> 00:19:36,003
path calculations and
how that BGP all works.

244
00:19:36,003 --> 00:19:40,824
And we only scratch the surface of BNGP,
but

245
00:19:40,824 --> 00:19:44,863
have a good sense of how this works and

246
00:19:44,863 --> 00:19:50,744
some of the kind of aspects
of how the internet works.